{"qid":"Q774","names":["Guatemala"],"title":"Guatemala","endonym":{"native":"República de Guatemala","lang":"es"},"capsule":"The Republic of Guatemala achieved independence from the Central American Federation in 1839 and has been an independent state since 1840. A U.S.-backed coup in 1954 overthrew the democratically elected President Jacobo Árbenz, ending a decade of reform and initiating decades of military rule and a civil war (1960–1996) that killed an estimated 200,000 people, the majority Maya civilians. A peace agreement signed in December 1996 ended the conflict; Guatemala has since maintained constitutional civilian government, though poverty, inequality, and emigration remain defining challenges.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Guatemala's contemporary and historical boundaries are well documented in diplomatic treaties and official surveys; territorial disputes with Belize over the Guatemalan claim persist as of the dataset end date but do not affect the core national territory.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Guatemala","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guatemala"},{"label":"Wikipedia: 1954 Guatemalan coup d'état","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1954_Guatemalan_coup_d%27%C3%A9tat"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Guatemalan Civil War","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guatemalan_Civil_War"}],"britannica":"place/Guatemala","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Guatemala","rulers":[{"qid":"Q734565","name":"Mariano Rivera Paz","start":1839,"end":1841,"image":"Mariano Rivera Paz.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mariano%20Rivera%20Paz.jpg?width=320","ima